menu "espbench configuration"

choice ESPBENCH_MODE
    prompt "Payload generation mode"
    default ESPBENCH_MODE_INCREMENTAL
    help
        How the single payload octet is produced for each message window.

        These map directly onto the three experiment shapes:

          - Static: one fixed carrier held for the whole run (a single mid,
            never rotated). Measures whether one unchanging beacon keeps being
            delivered by the relay over time.

          - Incremental: mid advances every update interval and the payload is
            (start + i) mod 256. A deterministic, self-checking ramp.

          - Random: mid advances every update interval and the payload is drawn
            from a seeded on-device PRNG (xorshift32). The host reproduces the
            exact same sequence from the seed, so correctness is still checkable.

    config ESPBENCH_MODE_STATIC
        bool "Static (single fixed carrier)"
    config ESPBENCH_MODE_INCREMENTAL
        bool "Incremental (payload = start + i)"
    config ESPBENCH_MODE_RANDOM
        bool "Random (seeded PRNG)"
endchoice

config ESPBENCH_MODE
    int
    # Values consumed by espbench.c to branch the sender.
    default 0 if ESPBENCH_MODE_STATIC
    default 1 if ESPBENCH_MODE_INCREMENTAL
    default 2 if ESPBENCH_MODE_RANDOM

config ESPBENCH_ADV_INTERVAL_MS
    int "BLE advertising interval (ms)"
    default 1000
    range 20 10240
    help
        How often a BLE legacy advertising event fires (passed to sm_ll_init).
        One event sweeps the three primary channels once, so this is roughly
        "one broadcast per channel per window". Range matches the BLE legacy
        non-connectable advertising limits (20 ms .. 10240 ms).

        This is the primary independent variable for throughput/deliverability:
        a shorter interval means more radiated advertisements per unit time and
        more chances for a passing relay to observe the carrier.

config ESPBENCH_UPDATE_INTERVAL_MS
    int "Payload/mid update interval (ms)"
    depends on !ESPBENCH_MODE_STATIC
    default 60000
    range 1000 3600000
    help
        Incremental/random only: how long each mid is held before rotating to
        the next (mid, payload). Should be comfortably larger than the
        advertising interval so every window radiates many advertisements. The
        relay network only files a report every minute or so, so windows much
        shorter than ~60 s will see poor deliverability by construction.

config ESPBENCH_MSG_COUNT
    int "Number of message windows to send"
    depends on !ESPBENCH_MODE_STATIC
    default 16
    range 1 100000
    help
        Incremental/random only: how many mids to broadcast, one per update
        interval, before the run finishes. Total run time is roughly
        MSG_COUNT * UPDATE_INTERVAL_MS.

config ESPBENCH_STATIC_DURATION_MS
    int "Static broadcast duration (ms)"
    depends on ESPBENCH_MODE_STATIC
    default 600000
    range 0 86400000
    help
        Static only: how long to hold the single carrier before the run reports
        itself finished. 0 means broadcast forever (the run never emits its done
        marker; useful for open-ended soak tests driven by hand).

config ESPBENCH_MID_BASE
    int "Starting message-id"
    default 0
    range 0 2000000000
    help
        The mid of the first (or only, in static mode) window. Every carrier is
        derived from (uid, mid, payload), so two matrix cells that reuse the same
        mid range on the same uid would broadcast colliding carriers on the
        shared relay. The run_matrix.py harness assigns each cell a disjoint mid
        base automatically; set this by hand only for standalone runs.

config ESPBENCH_PAYLOAD_START
    int "Starting payload byte"
    default 0
    range 0 255
    help
        Static: the constant payload octet. Incremental: the first octet, then
        +1 (mod 256) per window. Ignored in random mode.

config ESPBENCH_RANDOM_SEED
    hex "PRNG seed (random mode)"
    depends on ESPBENCH_MODE_RANDOM
    default 0x1
    range 0x1 0xFFFFFFFF
    help
        32-bit seed for the on-device xorshift32 PRNG used in random mode. The
        host harness reproduces the identical payload sequence from this value,
        so correctness stays verifiable. Must be non-zero (xorshift32 is stuck
        at zero); the range enforces this.

endmenu
